{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.observe\n     2\t  \"One observation step over a DaoStream reader: read a value, act on it, and\n     3\t   advance the cursor only if the act succeeded.\n     4\t\n     5\t   `step` owns no scheduler, loop, state, callback or policy.  It classifies\n     6\t   every outcome the contract declares as data and hands the caller what it\n     7\t   needs to decide: the successor cursor on progress, the recovery cursor on a\n     8\t   gap, the raw answer on a defect.  What to do about a gap, whether a defect\n     9\t   is fatal, how many values to take per turn, and what state to carry between\n    10\t   turns all belong to the caller.\n    11\t\n    12\t   The cursor advances in exactly one place -- after the effect has answered\n    13\t   `ok` -- so effect-before-commit is a property of this seam rather than a\n    14\t   rule each caller keeps.  An effect that throws propagates before any\n    15\t   successor exists, which gives a throwing effect the same guarantee for\n    16\t   free.\n    17\t\n    18\t   Callers are `dao.stream.forward` (effect: append to another stream),\n    19\t   `dao.stream.observer` (effect: load a program into a VM) and\n    20\t   `dao.jing` (effect: materialize content).  Each adds its own loop and its\n    21\t   own policy above this step.\n    22\t\n    23\t   The law every interpreter here obeys is that an element's cursor advances\n    24\t   exactly when its disposition has been durably recorded.  This step serves\n    25\t   the interpreters whose disposition is an external, refusable effect -- an\n    26\t   append, a load, a materialization -- where that means advancing only after\n    27\t   the effect answered ok.  Interpreters whose disposition is a local, total\n    28\t   state transition keep their own read loops: `dao.stream.rpc/poll!`,\n    29\t   `dao.stream.apply/serve-once!` and `yin.vm.engine`'s wait set thread\n    30\t   whole caller state under a budget with terminal short-circuits, and for\n    31\t   them advancing first and advancing after the commit are the same fact,\n    32\t   because no window exists in which one happened and the other did not.\n    33\t   `serve-once!` is the proof that this is one law and not two families: it\n    34\t   advances after delivery for a correlatable request, on the diagnostic for\n    35\t   an uncorrelatable one, and on terminal loss for an undeliverable response.\n    36\t\n    37\t   There is no ordering parameter here and none is needed.  A consumer that\n    38\t   wants an element consumed even when its interpretation is defective writes\n    39\t   a *total* effect -- one that classifies every element as data and so always\n    40\t   answers ok -- and gets consume-exactly-once through this same step.\"\n    41\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t(defn- valid-or-transport-error\n    45\t  \"Fold a defective host answer into the contract's outcome algebra.\n    46\t\n    47\t   A conforming operation already returns one of the contract outcomes. An\n    48\t   answer that is not a well-formed outcome map -- a non-map, a missing or\n    49\t   unqualified outcome keyword, a declared outcome missing its required\n    50\t   keys -- folds into `transport-error` with the raw answer retained, so\n    51\t   this step's branches stay total over whatever a composition supplies.\n    52\t   A well-formed outcome map whose outcome lies outside the operation's\n    53\t   declared set is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly:\n    54\t   it is preserved exactly as it arrived, and `step` classifies it as\n    55\t   `refused` (Result Convention, unrecognized outcomes).\"\n    56\t  [operation result]\n    57\t  (let [defect (stream/validate-outcome operation result)]\n    58\t    (if (or (nil? defect) (= :unauthorized-outcome (:error defect)))\n    59\t      result\n    60\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n    61\t       :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result\n    62\t       :dao.stream/answer result})))\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(defn step\n    66\t  \"Read one value from `source` at `cursor` and run `effect` on it.\n    67\t\n    68\t   `effect` is `(fn [value] -> outcome-map)` answering with the writer outcome\n    69\t   set: `ok`, `full` (\\\"not yet\\\"), `invalid-value`, `closed`, `refused`, or\n    70\t   `transport-error`.  An answer that is not a well-formed outcome map is\n    71\t   classified as `transport-error` with the raw answer retained.  A\n    72\t   well-formed outcome outside the declared set is unrecognized -- a newer\n    73\t   contract, not a defect -- and is preserved as it arrived: on a read it\n    74\t   classifies as `:refused`, on an effect as `:failed`, exactly as\n    75\t   `refused` classifies.  An effect that throws propagates.\n    76\t\n    77\t   Returns a map carrying `:cursor` and `:read` (the raw read answer) in every\n    78\t   case:\n    79\t\n    80\t   | status     | when                              | cursor    | also        |\n    81\t   | ---------- | --------------------------------- | --------- | ----------- |\n    82\t   | `:advance` | read ok, effect ok                | successor | `:effect`   |\n    83\t   | `:retry`   | read blocked, or effect full      | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    84\t   | `:ended`   | read end                          | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    85\t   | `:gap`     | read gap                          | unchanged | `:recovery` |\n    86\t   | `:refused` | read refused or unrecognized;     | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    87\t   |            | nothing was observed              |           |             |\n    88\t   | `:defect`  | read cursor-mismatch,             | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    89\t   |            | invalid-cursor, transport-error,  |           |             |\n    90\t   |            | or malformed                      |           |             |\n    91\t   | `:failed`  | effect invalid-value, closed,     | unchanged | `:outcome`, |\n    92\t   |            | refused, transport-error,         |           | `:effect`   |\n    93\t   |            | unrecognized, or malformed        |           |             |\n    94\t\n    95\t   A `:retry` from the effect also carries `:effect`.  The cursor is the\n    96\t   successor on `:advance` and unchanged on every other status.  `refused`\n    97\t   is a policy answer, not a defect: nothing was observed and nothing was\n    98\t   recorded, and the cursor stays where it was.  An unrecognized outcome\n    99\t   answers the same: a newer contract is a refusal, never a defect.\"\n   100\t  [source cursor effect]\n   101\t  (let [read-result (valid-or-transport-error :next (stream/next source cursor))\n   102\t        read-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   103\t    (case read-outcome\n   104\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   105\t      (let [effect-result (valid-or-transport-error\n   106\t                            :append!\n   107\t                            (effect (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n   108\t            effect-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome effect-result)]\n   109\t        (case effect-outcome\n   110\t          :dao.stream/ok {:status :advance\n   111\t                          :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)\n   112\t                          :read read-result\n   113\t                          :effect effect-result}\n   114\t          :dao.stream/full {:status :retry\n   115\t                            :cursor cursor\n   116\t                            :outcome effect-outcome\n   117\t                            :read read-result\n   118\t                            :effect effect-result}\n   119\t          {:status :failed\n   120\t           :cursor cursor\n   121\t           :outcome effect-outcome\n   122\t           :read read-result\n   123\t           :effect effect-result}))\n   124\t\n   125\t      :dao.stream/blocked {:status :retry\n   126\t                           :cursor cursor\n   127\t                           :outcome read-outcome\n   128\t                           :read read-result}\n   129\t\n   130\t      :dao.stream/end {:status :ended\n   131\t                       :cursor cursor\n   132\t                       :outcome read-outcome\n   133\t                       :read read-result}\n   134\t\n   135\t      :dao.stream/gap {:status :gap\n   136\t                       :cursor cursor\n   137\t                       :outcome read-outcome\n   138\t                       :recovery (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)\n   139\t                       :read read-result}\n   140\t\n   141\t      :dao.stream/refused {:status :refused\n   142\t                           :cursor cursor\n   143\t                           :outcome read-outcome\n   144\t                           :read read-result}\n   145\t\n   146\t      (:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n   147\t        :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n   148\t        :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   149\t      {:status :defect\n   150\t       :cursor cursor\n   151\t       :outcome read-outcome\n   152\t       :read read-result}\n   153\t\n   154\t      ;; A well-formed outcome outside this contract version, preserved by\n   155\t      ;; valid-or-transport-error: refused, never a defect.  Only a folded\n   156\t      ;; malformed answer carries transport-error, named in the clause\n   157\t      ;; above.\n   158\t      {:status :refused\n   159\t       :cursor cursor\n   160\t       :outcome read-outcome\n   161\t       :read read-result})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream\n     2\t  \"DaoStream v2: passive substrate for IO in datom.world.\n     3\t\n     4\t   Seven operations:\n     5\t     Five handle operations (protocols implemented per declared surface):\n     6\t       descriptor (universal reachability and identity projection)\n     7\t       cursor     (reader surface)\n     8\t       next       (reader surface)\n     9\t       append!    (writer surface)\n    10\t       close!     (closable surface)\n    11\t     Two transport entry functions (per-transport, host-composed):\n    12\t       create!    (creates a new logical stream from a creation specification)\n    13\t       attach!    (attaches to an existing stream via portable descriptor)\n    14\t\n    15\t   Result Convention:\n    16\t     Every operation returns an open outcome map keyed by :dao.stream/outcome.\n    17\t     All qualified keywords live under :dao.stream/…\n    18\t     Each operation has an exhaustive, closed outcome set.\"\n    19\t  (:refer-clojure :exclude [next]))\n    20\t\n    21\t\n    22\t;; =============================================================================\n    23\t;; The Seven Operations and Standard Keywords\n    24\t;; =============================================================================\n    25\t\n    26\t(def operations\n    27\t  \"The seven DaoStream v2 operations.\"\n    28\t  #{:create! :attach! :descriptor :cursor :next :append! :close!})\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t(def surfaces\n    32\t  \"The public operational surfaces a handle may declare.\"\n    33\t  #{:reader :writer :closable})\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\t;; Anchors\n    37\t(def anchor-oldest\n    38\t  \"Earliest retained position on the stream.\"\n    39\t  :dao.stream/oldest)\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t(def anchor-newest\n    43\t  \"Positioned after the newest appended value (observes next arrival).\"\n    44\t  :dao.stream/newest)\n    45\t\n    46\t\n    47\t(def standard-anchors\n    48\t  \"Contract-defined anchor keywords.\"\n    49\t  #{anchor-oldest anchor-newest})\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t;; Closed outcome sets per operation (contract exhaustive tables)\n    53\t(def outcomes-create\n    54\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    55\t    :dao.stream/invalid-spec\n    56\t    :dao.stream/not-found\n    57\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(def outcomes-attach\n    61\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    62\t    :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor\n    63\t    :dao.stream/not-found\n    64\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(def outcomes-descriptor\n    68\t  #{:dao.stream/ok})\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(def outcomes-cursor\n    72\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    73\t    :dao.stream/invalid-anchor\n    74\t    :dao.stream/closed\n    75\t    :dao.stream/refused\n    76\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    77\t\n    78\t\n    79\t(def outcomes-next\n    80\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    81\t    :dao.stream/blocked\n    82\t    :dao.stream/end\n    83\t    :dao.stream/gap\n    84\t    :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n    85\t    :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n    86\t    :dao.stream/refused\n    87\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(def outcomes-append\n    91\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    92\t    :dao.stream/full\n    93\t    :dao.stream/invalid-value\n    94\t    :dao.stream/closed\n    95\t    :dao.stream/refused\n    96\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t(def outcomes-close\n   100\t  #{:dao.stream/ok})\n   101\t\n   102\t\n   103\t(def operation-outcomes\n   104\t  \"Map of operation keyword to its closed outcome set.\"\n   105\t  {:create! outcomes-create\n   106\t   :attach! outcomes-attach\n   107\t   :descriptor outcomes-descriptor\n   108\t   :cursor outcomes-cursor\n   109\t   :next outcomes-next\n   110\t   :append! outcomes-append\n   111\t   :close! outcomes-close})\n   112\t\n   113\t\n   114\t(def outcome-required-keys\n   115\t  \"Map from [operation outcome] to required keys in the result map.\"\n   116\t  {[:create! :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/handle}\n   117\t   [:attach! :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/handle}\n   118\t   [:descriptor :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/descriptor :dao.stream/identity}\n   119\t   [:cursor :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/cursor}\n   120\t   [:next :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/value :dao.stream/cursor}\n   121\t   [:next :dao.stream/gap] #{:dao.stream/cursor}})\n   122\t\n   123\t\n   124\t;; =============================================================================\n   125\t;; Canonical Operation Resolution Helper\n   126\t;; =============================================================================\n   127\t\n   128\t(defn canonical-op\n   129\t  \"Normalizes op to canonical keyword (:create!, :attach!, :descriptor,\n   130\t   :cursor, :next, :append!, :close!). Supports aliases without '!'.\"\n   131\t  [op]\n   132\t  (case op\n   133\t    (:create :create!) :create!\n   134\t    (:attach :attach!) :attach!\n   135\t    (:descriptor) :descriptor\n   136\t    (:cursor) :cursor\n   137\t    (:next) :next\n   138\t    (:append :append!) :append!\n   139\t    (:close :close!) :close!\n   140\t    nil))\n   141\t\n   142\t\n   143\t;; =============================================================================\n   144\t;; Handle Protocols (Five Handle Operations)\n   145\t;; =============================================================================\n   146\t\n   147\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamDescriptor\n   148\t  \"Universal reachability and identity projection. Belongs to no surface:\n   149\t   implemented by every handle regardless of declared reader/writer/closable surfaces.\"\n   150\t\n   151\t  (descriptor\n   152\t    [handle]\n   153\t    \"Returns descriptor outcome map:\n   154\t     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   155\t      :dao.stream/descriptor <portable-descriptor>\n   156\t      :dao.stream/identity <logical-stream-identity>}\"))\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamReader\n   160\t  \"Reader surface: positioned, non-destructive observation via immutable cursors.\"\n   161\t\n   162\t  (cursor\n   163\t    [handle anchor]\n   164\t    \"Mint an immutable cursor from anchor (:dao.stream/oldest, :dao.stream/newest,\n   165\t     or transport-specific anchor).\n   166\t     Returns outcome map with :dao.stream/cursor on :dao.stream/ok.\")\n   167\t\n   168\t  (next\n   169\t    [handle cursor]\n   170\t    \"Read the value at cursor position. Total and non-blocking.\n   171\t     Returns read outcome map.\"))\n   172\t\n   173\t\n   174\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamWriter\n   175\t  \"Writer surface: append values to the sequence this handle's writer surface is on.\"\n   176\t\n   177\t  (append!\n   178\t    [handle val]\n   179\t    \"Append val to the sequence. Total and non-blocking.\n   180\t     Returns write outcome map.\"))\n   181\t\n   182\t\n   183\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamClosable\n   184\t  \"Closable surface: close what this handle is on (logical stream or attachment).\n   185\t   Idempotent.\"\n   186\t\n   187\t  (close!\n   188\t    [handle]\n   189\t    \"Closes what this handle is on. Irrevocable.\n   190\t     Returns outcome map {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}.\"))\n   191\t\n   192\t\n   193\t;; =============================================================================\n   194\t;; Surface Inspection & Gating\n   195\t;; =============================================================================\n   196\t\n   197\t(defn reader?\n   198\t  \"True if handle implements the reader surface (IDaoStreamReader).\"\n   199\t  [handle]\n   200\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamReader handle))\n   201\t\n   202\t\n   203\t(defn writer?\n   204\t  \"True if handle implements the writer surface (IDaoStreamWriter).\"\n   205\t  [handle]\n   206\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamWriter handle))\n   207\t\n   208\t\n   209\t(defn closable?\n   210\t  \"True if handle implements the closable surface (IDaoStreamClosable).\"\n   211\t  [handle]\n   212\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamClosable handle))\n   213\t\n   214\t\n   215\t(defn descriptor?\n   216\t  \"True if handle implements IDaoStreamDescriptor.\"\n   217\t  [handle]\n   218\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamDescriptor handle))\n   219\t\n   220\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '300,500p;1360,1460p'\"","aggregated_output":"   300\t(defn attach-resource\n   301\t  \"Install the host stream `handle` a composition hands a task under a\n   302\t   fresh resource id, and return `[stream-ref state]` with the reference\n   303\t   sealed under the task's secret. This is how a stream reaches a program:\n   304\t   never through the store.\"\n   305\t  [state handle]\n   306\t  (let [[id state] (gensym state \"stream\")\n   307\t        ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)]\n   308\t    [ref (assoc-in state [:resources id] handle)]))\n   309\t\n   310\t\n   311\t(def ^:private wake-error-outcomes\n   312\t  \"The declared outcomes `handle-next` and `handle-put` raise as errors,\n   313\t   by wait reason: the read defects for a parked reader, the write defects\n   314\t   for a parked writer. A wake raises these exactly as the immediate\n   315\t   operation would. Every other status is either a value answer or an\n   316\t   outcome outside this contract version, and both of those the immediate\n   317\t   path answers as data -- so the wake answers them as data too.\"\n   318\t  {:next #{:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n   319\t           :dao.stream/transport-error}\n   320\t   :put #{:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/invalid-value\n   321\t          :dao.stream/transport-error}})\n   322\t\n   323\t\n   324\t(def ^:private wake-value-statuses\n   325\t  \"The statuses a woken entry resumes with its poll's own value, by wait\n   326\t   reason: ok, end and gap for a parked reader, ok alone for a parked\n   327\t   writer -- the write outcome set has no end and no gap, so those\n   328\t   keywords on an append are outcomes outside this contract version and\n   329\t   refuse. An entry that was never polled carries no status and resumes\n   330\t   with the value it already holds.\"\n   331\t  {:next #{nil :ok :end :dao.stream/gap}\n   332\t   :put #{nil :ok}})\n   333\t\n   334\t\n   335\t(defn- wake-refusal?\n   336\t  \"True when a woken `:next`/`:put` entry's `status` is one the immediate\n   337\t   path answers as the shaped refusal: `:dao.stream/refused`, or a\n   338\t   well-formed outcome outside this contract version -- a newer contract,\n   339\t   not a defect. The declared error outcomes are terminal instead, and\n   340\t   the value statuses carry their poll's own value.\"\n   341\t  [reason status]\n   342\t  (and (contains? #{:next :put} reason)\n   343\t       (not (contains? (get wake-value-statuses reason) status))\n   344\t       (not (contains? (get wake-error-outcomes reason) status))))\n   345\t\n   346\t\n   347\t(defn make-woken-run-queue-entries\n   348\t  \"Transform woken wait-set entries into ready-queue entries.\n   349\t   Readers (with :cursor-ref) record the successor cursor the transport\n   350\t   returned as a resource update. Writers (no :cursor-ref) just stamp\n   351\t   :value. Each woken result's :status rides onto the ready entry, where\n   352\t   terminal-resume-outcome reads it.\n   353\t\n   354\t   A woken status the immediate `handle-put`/`handle-next` answers as the\n   355\t   shaped refusal -- `:dao.stream/refused`, or an outcome outside this\n   356\t   contract version -- is shaped into that same refusal value, so whether\n   357\t   the first attempt blocked does not change what the program sees. The\n   358\t   declared error outcomes stay raw, for terminal-resume-outcome to raise.\n   359\t\n   360\t   The ready entry stays pure data: any `:stream` handle a poll resolved\n   361\t   is dropped -- the resource updates carry the successor cursor, and\n   362\t   handles are re-resolved from ids when needed again -- so an entry\n   363\t   neither waits nor runs holding a host object.\"\n   364\t  [state woken]\n   365\t  (mapv (fn [{:keys [entry value cursor status], :as woken-entry}]\n   366\t          (let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)\n   367\t                value (if (wake-refusal? (:reason entry) status)\n   368\t                        (cond-> {:status :refused\n   369\t                                 :outcome status\n   370\t                                 :stream-id (:stream-id entry)}\n   371\t                          cursor-ref (assoc :cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)))\n   372\t                        value)\n   373\t                updates\n   374\t                (or (:resource-updates woken-entry)\n   375\t                    (when (and cursor-ref cursor)\n   376\t                      (let [cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n   377\t                            cursor-data (get (:resources state) cursor-id)]\n   378\t                        {cursor-id (assoc cursor-data :cursor cursor)})))]\n   379\t            (dissoc (assoc entry\n   380\t                           :value value\n   381\t                           :resource-updates updates\n   382\t                           :status status\n   383\t                           :cursor cursor)\n   384\t                    :stream)))\n   385\t        woken))\n   386\t\n   387\t\n   388\t(defn handle-make\n   389\t  \"Handle :stream/make. Creates a stream through the composition's\n   390\t   `:make-stream`, holds the handle in the private `:resources` table, and\n   391\t   answers a sealed reference; the reference is issued first, so a task\n   392\t   with no secret creates no stream.\n   393\t\n   394\t   A nil capacity has no v2 meaning, so the module path defaults exactly as\n   395\t   the AST path does. Absent a supplied constructor this fails and says so;\n   396\t   silently falling back to a private transport is the failure mode the rule\n   397\t   exists to prevent.\n   398\t   Returns [stream-ref updated-state].\"\n   399\t  [state effect id]\n   400\t  (let [capacity (or (:capacity effect) vm/default-stream-capacity)\n   401\t        stream-ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)\n   402\t        handle (vm/create-stream! (:make-stream state) capacity :stream/make)]\n   403\t    [stream-ref (assoc-in state [:resources id] handle)]))\n   404\t\n   405\t\n   406\t(defn handle-put\n   407\t  \"Handle :stream/put. Total over the six append outcomes, once the\n   408\t   reference verifies.\n   409\t   Returns {:value v :state s} on success, {:park true :stream-id id :state s}\n   410\t   on `full`. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are errors\n   411\t   that name their outcome, as v1's throw on a closed stream did.\n   412\t   `refused`, and any outcome outside this contract version, is not a\n   413\t   throw: the effect's value is the shaped refusal\n   414\t   {:status :refused, :outcome o, :stream-id id} -- data the program\n   415\t   reads, the way `poll-link-response` answers a refused link.\"\n   416\t  [state effect]\n   417\t  (let [stream-id (check-ref! state :stream/put :stream-ref (:stream effect))\n   418\t        val (:val effect)\n   419\t        handle (get (:resources state) stream-id)\n   420\t        result (stream/append! handle val)\n   421\t        o (outcome result)]\n   422\t    (case o\n   423\t      :dao.stream/ok {:value val, :state state}\n   424\t      :dao.stream/full {:park true, :stream-id stream-id, :state state}\n   425\t      (:dao.stream/closed\n   426\t        :dao.stream/invalid-value\n   427\t        :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   428\t      (fail \"Stream append failed\" {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id})\n   429\t      {:state state,\n   430\t       :value {:status :refused, :outcome o, :stream-id stream-id}})))\n   431\t\n   432\t\n   433\t(defn handle-cursor\n   434\t  \"Handle :stream/cursor. Mints an opaque cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\n   435\t   into a cursor cell in `:resources`, and answers a sealed reference.\n   436\t\n   437\t   v1 fabricated `{:position 0}` and touched no stream. Minting is a stream\n   438\t   operation, so `closed` and `transport-error` arrive here.\n   439\t   Returns [cursor-ref updated-state].\"\n   440\t  [state effect id]\n   441\t  (let [stream-id (check-ref! state :stream/cursor :stream-ref (:stream effect))\n   442\t        cursor-ref (issue-ref state :cursor-ref id)\n   443\t        cursor (vm/mint-oldest (get (:resources state) stream-id)\n   444\t                               :stream/cursor)]\n   445\t    [cursor-ref\n   446\t     (assoc-in state [:resources id] (vm/cursor-entry stream-id cursor))]))\n   447\t\n   448\t\n   449\t(defn handle-next\n   450\t  \"Handle :stream/next. Total over the eight read outcomes, once the\n   451\t   reference verifies.\n   452\t\n   453\t   `ok` advances the cursor cell to the exact returned successor. `blocked`\n   454\t   parks. `end` yields nil, as v1 did. `gap` advances to the recovery cursor\n   455\t   and yields `:dao.stream/gap`, so a program that reads it learns values were\n   456\t   lost. `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` and `transport-error` are\n   457\t   errors. `refused`, and any outcome outside this contract version, is\n   458\t   not a throw: the effect's value is the shaped refusal\n   459\t   {:status :refused, :outcome o, :stream-id id, :cursor-id cid} --\n   460\t   data the program reads, the way `poll-link-response` answers a\n   461\t   refused link.\"\n   462\t  [state effect]\n   463\t  (let [cursor-ref (:cursor effect)\n   464\t        cursor-id (check-ref! state :stream/next :cursor-ref cursor-ref)\n   465\t        resources (:resources state)\n   466\t        cursor-data (get resources cursor-id)\n   467\t        stream-id (:stream-id cursor-data)\n   468\t        handle (get resources stream-id)]\n   469\t    (when (nil? handle)\n   470\t      (fail \"Stream not found for cursor\" {:stream-id stream-id}))\n   471\t    (let [result (stream/next handle (:cursor cursor-data))\n   472\t          o (outcome result)\n   473\t          advance (fn [value]\n   474\t                    {:value value,\n   475\t                     :state (assoc-in state\n   476\t                                      [:resources cursor-id :cursor]\n   477\t                                      (:dao.stream/cursor result))})]\n   478\t      (case o\n   479\t        :dao.stream/ok (advance (:dao.stream/value result))\n   480\t        :dao.stream/blocked {:park true,\n   481\t                             :cursor-ref cursor-ref,\n   482\t                             :stream-id stream-id,\n   483\t                             :state state}\n   484\t        :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :state state}\n   485\t        :dao.stream/gap (advance :dao.stream/gap)\n   486\t        (:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n   487\t          :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n   488\t          :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   489\t        (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n   490\t              {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id, :cursor-id cursor-id})\n   491\t        {:state state,\n   492\t         :value {:status :refused,\n   493\t                 :outcome o,\n   494\t                 :stream-id stream-id,\n   495\t                 :cursor-id cursor-id}}))))\n   496\t\n   497\t\n   498\t(defn handle-close\n   499\t  \"Handle :stream/close, once the reference verifies. `close!` is total\n   500\t   over {ok} and wakes nothing: a reader parked on this stream learns of\n  1360\t        (update v\n  1361\t                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n  1362\t                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n  1363\t\n  1364\t\n  1365\t(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n  1366\t\n  1367\t\n  1368\t(defn run-loop\n  1369\t  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support. Ready entries are pure data;\n  1370\t   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n  1371\t  [state active? step-fn resume-fn]\n  1372\t  (loop [v state]\n  1373\t    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n  1374\t      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n  1375\t            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v)]\n  1376\t                            (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v')]\n  1377\t                              (recur resumed)\n  1378\t                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n  1379\t            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v)]\n  1380\t                      (recur resumed)\n  1381\t                      v)\n  1382\t            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n  1383\t\n  1384\t\n  1385\t(def ^:private waitset-diagnostics\n  1386\t  \"The statuses `dao.stream.waitset/check` wakes an entry it could not poll\n  1387\t   at all with: an unsupported reason, an unresolvable entry, or an answer —\n  1388\t   or transport — it could not interpret. No park site in `yin.vm` produces\n  1389\t   an entry that earns one, and an entry that earns one must not reach a\n  1390\t   restore function as a value.\"\n  1391\t  #{:dao.stream.waitset/unsupported-reason\n  1392\t    :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved\n  1393\t    :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer})\n  1394\t\n  1395\t\n  1396\t(defn- terminal-resume-outcome\n  1397\t  \"The outcome a woken entry resolved with, when it is one the immediate\n  1398\t   path raises as an error. `handle-put` and `handle-next` raise exactly\n  1399\t   the declared error outcomes of their operation, and whether the first\n  1400\t   attempt blocked must not change that. An outcome outside this contract\n  1401\t   version is the shaped refusal on the immediate path, so it is the\n  1402\t   shaped refusal here too, never a raise. A waitset diagnostic is\n  1403\t   terminal whatever the entry's `:reason` -- an unsupported reason is by\n  1404\t   definition outside the `#{:next :put}` this check otherwise reads --\n  1405\t   so it is named here rather than falling through to the restore. An\n  1406\t   entry that was never polled carries no status.\"\n  1407\t  [entry]\n  1408\t  (let [status (:status entry)]\n  1409\t    (cond\n  1410\t      (contains? waitset-diagnostics status) status\n  1411\t      (= :link-refused status) status\n  1412\t      (contains? #{:next :put} (:reason entry))\n  1413\t      (when (contains? (get wake-error-outcomes (:reason entry)) status)\n  1414\t        status))))\n  1415\t\n  1416\t\n  1417\t(defn- throw-terminal-resume!\n  1418\t  \"Fail a resumed entry exactly as the immediate operation would have. A\n  1419\t   waitset diagnostic names its status and the entry that earned it: there\n  1420\t   is no stream operation whose error vocabulary it belongs to.\"\n  1421\t  [entry o]\n  1422\t  (cond\n  1423\t    (contains? waitset-diagnostics o)\n  1424\t    (fail \"Wait-set entry woke with a diagnostic\" {:status o, :entry entry})\n  1425\t    ;; a refused or lost link, or a refused install: the effect's error\n  1426\t    (= :link-refused o)\n  1427\t    (fail (str \"Module link refused: \"\n  1428\t               (clojure.core/name (or (:reason (:refusal entry)) :refused)))\n  1429\t          (assoc (:refusal entry) :link-module (:name entry)))\n  1430\t    (= :next (:reason entry))\n  1431\t    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n  1432\t          {:outcome o,\n  1433\t           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n  1434\t           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n  1435\t    :else (fail \"Stream append failed\"\n  1436\t                {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n  1437\t\n  1438\t\n  1439\t(defn resume-from-run-queue\n  1440\t  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge its resource updates into\n  1441\t   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n  1442\t\n  1443\t   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n  1444\t   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n  1445\t   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n  1446\t   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n  1447\t   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\n  1448\t\n  1449\t   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where