{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '347,395p;1450,1485p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '100,125p'\"","aggregated_output":"   347\t(defn make-woken-run-queue-entries\n   348\t  \"Transform woken wait-set entries into ready-queue entries.\n   349\t   Readers (with :cursor-ref) record the successor cursor the transport\n   350\t   returned as a resource update. Writers (no :cursor-ref) just stamp\n   351\t   :value. Each woken result's :status rides onto the ready entry, where\n   352\t   terminal-resume-outcome reads it.\n   353\t\n   354\t   A woken status the immediate `handle-put`/`handle-next` answers as the\n   355\t   shaped refusal -- `:dao.stream/refused`, or an outcome outside this\n   356\t   contract version -- is shaped into that same refusal value, so whether\n   357\t   the first attempt blocked does not change what the program sees. The\n   358\t   declared error outcomes stay raw, for terminal-resume-outcome to raise.\n   359\t\n   360\t   The ready entry stays pure data: any `:stream` handle a poll resolved\n   361\t   is dropped -- the resource updates carry the successor cursor, and\n   362\t   handles are re-resolved from ids when needed again -- so an entry\n   363\t   neither waits nor runs holding a host object.\"\n   364\t  [state woken]\n   365\t  (mapv (fn [{:keys [entry value cursor status], :as woken-entry}]\n   366\t          (let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)\n   367\t                value (if (wake-refusal? (:reason entry) status)\n   368\t                        (cond-> {:status :refused\n   369\t                                 :outcome status\n   370\t                                 :stream-id (:stream-id entry)}\n   371\t                          cursor-ref (assoc :cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)))\n   372\t                        value)\n   373\t                updates\n   374\t                (or (:resource-updates woken-entry)\n   375\t                    (when (and cursor-ref cursor)\n   376\t                      (let [cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n   377\t                            cursor-data (get (:resources state) cursor-id)]\n   378\t                        {cursor-id (assoc cursor-data :cursor cursor)})))]\n   379\t            (dissoc (assoc entry\n   380\t                           :value value\n   381\t                           :resource-updates updates\n   382\t                           :status status\n   383\t                           :cursor cursor)\n   384\t                    :stream)))\n   385\t        woken))\n   386\t\n   387\t\n   388\t(defn handle-make\n   389\t  \"Handle :stream/make. Creates a stream through the composition's\n   390\t   `:make-stream`, holds the handle in the private `:resources` table, and\n   391\t   answers a sealed reference; the reference is issued first, so a task\n   392\t   with no secret creates no stream.\n   393\t\n   394\t   A nil capacity has no v2 meaning, so the module path defaults exactly as\n   395\t   the AST path does. Absent a supplied constructor this fails and says so;\n  1450\t   `val` is the woken `:value` — the same signature `resume-continuation`\n  1451\t   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n  1452\t   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 2).\"\n  1453\t  [state restore-fn]\n  1454\t  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n  1455\t    (when (seq run-queue)\n  1456\t      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n  1457\t            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n  1458\t            updates (:resource-updates entry)\n  1459\t            ;; a woken entry only ever carries engine-minted keyword keys\n  1460\t            ;; (cursor ids)\n  1461\t            _ (when-let [k (some #(when-not (keyword? %) %) (keys updates))]\n  1462\t                (fail \"Ready entry resource update carries a non-minted key\"\n  1463\t                      {:rule :resource-update-key, :key k}))\n  1464\t            base (assoc state\n  1465\t                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n  1466\t                        :resources (merge (:resources state) updates)\n  1467\t                        :blocked? false\n  1468\t                        :halted? false)]\n  1469\t        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n  1470\t          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n  1471\t          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n  1472\t\n  1473\t\n  1474\t(defn scheduler-round\n  1475\t  \"One round between continuations: poll the wait set, then resume whatever\n  1476\t   woke, through `restore-fn` (`base entry val -> state`, the one restore\n  1477\t   signature `yin.vm.engine.md` §2.1 states). Returns the polled state when\n  1478\t   nothing woke, so a caller always gets a state back.\n  1479\t\n  1480\t   This is `yin.vm.semantic/scheduler-round` with its restore made a\n  1481\t   parameter (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 1); every VM was writing this\n  1482\t   composition by hand.\"\n  1483\t  [state restore-fn]\n  1484\t  (let [v' (check-wait-set state)]\n  1485\t    (or (resume-from-run-queue v' restore-fn) v')))\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Each outcome set is exhaustive **for this version of the contract**. A\n   114\t  consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   115\t  `ok`; nothing observed; for a write, effect unknown and no automatic\n   116\t  retry; and not retryable unless the result map carries\n   117\t  `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an unrecognized\n   118\t  outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly. A\n   119\t  transport may carry `:dao.stream/retry? true` on any non-`ok` outcome to\n   120\t  say that asking again is the right response.\n   121\t- `:dao.stream/refused` is an outcome `cursor`, `next` and `append!` may\n   122\t  produce: a policy composed on this handle declined the operation; nothing\n   123\t  was observed and nothing was appended; not retryable unless the map\n   124\t  carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. `descriptor` and `close!` never produce\n   125\t  it: a name is not gated and a lifecycle transition is not declined. What\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/waitset.cljc | sed -n '1,290p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.waitset\n     2\t  \"The multiplexed wait-set for DaoStream: one complete ordered sweep over\n     3\t  every parked waiter, as a host-agnostic library.\n     4\t\n     5\t  The waitset is a value, `{:waiting []}`, and it holds only what waits.\n     6\t  `check` takes it beside a host-composed resolver and the host's store,\n     7\t  performs one synchronous interpreter step — polling live media, appending\n     8\t  for a parked writer — and returns the successor waitset beside that\n     9\t  call's results. The sweep has explicit stream effects, so it is never\n    10\t  described as pure; what is immutable is the threading of the store and\n    11\t  the waitset.\n    12\t\n    13\t  Entries are the host's data. The library interprets only `:reason` —\n    14\t  `:next` reads, `:put` appends — and every other field is opaque, stored\n    15\t  and returned exactly as the host gave it. A resolver maps each whole\n    16\t  entry to a live handle and the cursor to read from or the value to\n    17\t  append, and moves a reader's cursor in the store; both functions run\n    18\t  immediately inside `check` and are never retained, and the library never\n    19\t  adds a resolved handle or polling cursor to any entry.\n    20\t\n    21\t  Woken entries are returned, never dispatched: no callbacks, no queues,\n    22\t  no disposition functions. This namespace multiplexes readiness; it does\n    23\t  not schedule work — the host loop owns everything between calls.\"\n    24\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    25\t\n    26\t\n    27\t(def ^:private wait-reasons\n    28\t  \"The reasons the library interprets. Only two outcomes can change on\n    29\t   their own: `blocked` for a reader, `full` for a writer.\"\n    30\t  #{:next :put})\n    31\t\n    32\t\n    33\t(defn empty-waitset\n    34\t  \"The waitset that parks nothing. This value is the whole threaded state.\"\n    35\t  []\n    36\t  {:waiting []})\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(defn park\n    40\t  \"Append `entry` to `waitset`'s `:waiting` and return the successor\n    41\t   waitset. Total: it rejects nothing, because `check` classifies\n    42\t   everything — a malformed entry is woken by the sweep as a diagnostic,\n    43\t   never a park-time error. Total over the waitset it is handed too: a\n    44\t   missing or nil `:waiting` parks onto a fresh vector, so park order —\n    45\t   wake order among co-waiters — is preserved whatever the host threaded.\"\n    46\t  [waitset entry]\n    47\t  (update waitset :waiting (fnil conj []) entry))\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\t(defn- woken-result\n    51\t  \"The result map for one woken entry: the stored entry verbatim beside\n    52\t   the status its poll resolved with. `cursor` is present only when the\n    53\t   poll produced one — a reader's successor or a gap's recovery position;\n    54\t   a writer resolves with no cursor.\"\n    55\t  [entry status value cursor]\n    56\t  (cond-> {:entry entry :status status :value value}\n    57\t    cursor (assoc :cursor cursor)))\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(defn- diagnostic-result\n    61\t  \"The result map for an entry the library cannot poll at all: the stored\n    62\t   entry verbatim and its qualified status. No stream operation was\n    63\t   performed, so there is no value.\"\n    64\t  [entry status]\n    65\t  {:entry entry :status status})\n    66\t\n    67\t\n    68\t(defn- answer-defect\n    69\t  \"Why `answer` cannot be interpreted as an outcome of `op`, or nil when it\n    70\t   can, using the contract's own validation. `:invalid` marks an answer\n    71\t   that is not an outcome map the contract can vouch for — a non-map, a\n    72\t   missing or unqualified `:dao.stream/outcome`, or a declared outcome\n    73\t   missing its required keys — which folds into the waitset's own\n    74\t   diagnostic. `:unauthorized` marks a well-formed outcome outside the\n    75\t   operation's declared set, which stays terminal under its own keyword\n    76\t   (the plan's rule for undeclared outcomes) and is distinguished here so\n    77\t   it does not collide with the fold.\"\n    78\t  [op answer]\n    79\t  (let [defect (stream/validate-outcome op answer)]\n    80\t    (cond\n    81\t      (nil? defect) nil\n    82\t      (= :unauthorized-outcome (:error defect)) :unauthorized\n    83\t      :else :invalid)))\n    84\t\n    85\t\n    86\t(defn- poll-next\n    87\t  \"Poll one resolved `:next` entry with a synchronous read. Returns nil\n    88\t   while the entry must keep waiting — `blocked` is the only reader\n    89\t   outcome that can change on its own — and otherwise the woken result\n    90\t   plus `:advance-cursor`, the position the store must move to before\n    91\t   later entries resolve: the successor on `ok`, the recovery position on\n    92\t   `gap`. `end` resolves as the immediate path yields it, and every other\n    93\t   outcome — cursor-mismatch, invalid-cursor, transport-error, and\n    94\t   anything undeclared — is terminal under its own keyword.\n    95\t\n    96\t   An answer that is not an outcome map the contract can vouch for — a\n    97\t   non-map, a missing or unqualified outcome, a declared outcome missing\n    98\t   its required keys — folds into the qualified terminal status\n    99\t   `:dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer` instead of waking as an\n   100\t   unnameable `:status nil`. A poll that throws — a `:stream` that fails\n   101\t   its protocol, a defective transport — folds into the same diagnostic:\n   102\t   the entry cannot be trusted to poll again, and an uninterpretable\n   103\t   answer is terminal data, never an exception and never a wait.\"\n   104\t  [entry resolved]\n   105\t  (try\n   106\t    (let [result (stream/next (:stream resolved) (:cursor resolved))\n   107\t          o (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n   108\t      (if (= :invalid (answer-defect :next result))\n   109\t        {:wake (diagnostic-result entry :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer)}\n   110\t        (case o\n   111\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   112\t          (let [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)]\n   113\t            {:wake (woken-result entry :ok (:dao.stream/value result) cursor)\n   114\t             :advance-cursor cursor})\n   115\t\n   116\t          :dao.stream/blocked nil\n   117\t\n   118\t          :dao.stream/end\n   119\t          {:wake (woken-result entry :end nil nil)}\n   120\t\n   121\t          :dao.stream/gap\n   122\t          (let [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)]\n   123\t            {:wake (woken-result entry :dao.stream/gap :dao.stream/gap cursor)\n   124\t             :advance-cursor cursor})\n   125\t\n   126\t          ;; Terminal under its own keyword, never a wait: waiting on an\n   127\t          ;; answer the library cannot interpret would spin.\n   128\t          {:wake (woken-result entry o o nil)})))\n   129\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   130\t      {:wake (diagnostic-result entry :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer)})))\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t(defn- poll-put\n   134\t  \"Poll one resolved `:put` entry by appending — the append is an effect\n   135\t   of this poll, and `check` returns the state that records it. Returns\n   136\t   nil while the entry must keep waiting — `full` is the only writer\n   137\t   outcome that can change on its own — and otherwise the woken result.\n   138\t   `ok` resolves with the appended value and no cursor; `closed` is a\n   139\t   terminal append failure under its own keyword, never a reader's `end`;\n   140\t   every other outcome, declared or not, is terminal under its own\n   141\t   keyword.\n   142\t\n   143\t   A resolution with no `:value` appends nil: the transport is the one\n   144\t   that answers `invalid-value`, and the engine reads its `:datom` the\n   145\t   same loose way. An answer that is not an outcome map, and a poll that\n   146\t   throws — a `:stream` failing its writer protocol, a defective\n   147\t   transport — fold into `:dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer`, exactly as\n   148\t   the reader's poll does: the entry cannot be trusted to poll again.\"\n   149\t  [entry resolved]\n   150\t  (try\n   151\t    (let [value (:value resolved)\n   152\t          result (stream/append! (:stream resolved) value)\n   153\t          o (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n   154\t      (if (= :invalid (answer-defect :append! result))\n   155\t        {:wake (diagnostic-result entry :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer)}\n   156\t        (case o\n   157\t          :dao.stream/ok {:wake (woken-result entry :ok value nil)}\n   158\t          :dao.stream/full nil\n   159\t          {:wake (woken-result entry o o nil)})))\n   160\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   161\t      {:wake (diagnostic-result entry :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer)})))\n   162\t\n   163\t\n   164\t(defn check\n   165\t  \"Run one complete ordered sweep over `waitset`'s entries: a synchronous,\n   166\t  state-threaded interpreter step with explicit stream effects — it calls\n   167\t  `next` on live media and a parked writer's `append!` — so it is never\n   168\t  described as pure.\n   169\t\n   170\t  `resolver` is host composition, two synchronous functions over whole\n   171\t  entries:\n   172\t\n   173\t    {:resolve (fn [store entry] ...)          ; => {:stream h :cursor c :value v} or nil\n   174\t     :advance (fn [store entry cursor] ...)}  ; => store'\n   175\t\n   176\t  `:resolve` maps an entry to its live handle plus the cursor to read from\n   177\t  (`:next`) or the value to append (`:put`), or answers nil when it\n   178\t  cannot. `:advance` returns the store with that entry's reader cursor\n   179\t  moved to `cursor`. Both are invoked immediately, per entry, and never\n   180\t  retained — the shape of `reduce`'s `f`, not an event callback. The store\n   181\t  is opaque to this library: it is never dereferenced here, only passed\n   182\t  through.\n   183\t\n   184\t  Returns\n   185\t\n   186\t    {:waitset {:waiting [...]}   ; thread this into the next check\n   187\t     :woken   [...]              ; this call's results only; host-owned\n   188\t     :store   store'}            ; the host commits this\n   189\t\n   190\t  Every entry is polled every call, one at a time in wait-set order — no\n   191\t  budget, no scan position. A still-waiting entry (`blocked` for a reader,\n   192\t  `full` for a writer) is retained exactly as stored. A resolved entry\n   193\t  leaves `:waiting` and appears in `:woken` as\n   194\t\n   195\t    {:entry <the stored entry> :status ... :value ... :cursor ...}\n   196\t\n   197\t  where `:cursor` is present only for a reader whose poll returned one.\n   198\t  The library never adds a resolved handle or polling cursor to any entry\n   199\t  it retains or returns, and `:woken` never accumulates: check the\n   200\t  returned `:waitset` again and it answers only what moved since.\n   201\t\n   202\t  Two laws are preserved from the engine this sweep generalizes.\n   203\t  Shared-cursor write-back: a woken reader's successor — or recovery\n   204\t  cursor, on gap — is advanced in the store before later entries resolve,\n   205\t  so co-waiters on one cursor read distinct values and a co-waiter behind\n   206\t  a gap reads from the recovery position; wait-set order is therefore wake\n   207\t  order, and nothing reorders `:waiting`. A parked writer retries by\n   208\t  appending: the append is an effect of the poll, so no caller may drop a\n   209\t  returned `:waitset` or `:store`.\n   210\t\n   211\t  Classification is total, per operation. For a `:next` entry only\n   212\t  `blocked` waits: `ok` resolves with the value and its successor, `end`\n   213\t  as `{:value nil :status :end}`, `gap` with its recovery cursor, and\n   214\t  cursor-mismatch, invalid-cursor and transport-error under their own\n   215\t  qualified keywords. For a `:put` entry only `full` waits: `ok` resolves\n   216\t  with the appended value and no cursor; closed, invalid-value and\n   217\t  transport-error are terminal append failures under their own keywords —\n   218\t  writer `closed` is never a reader's `end`. Any outcome outside the\n   219\t  operation's declared set is terminal under its own keyword, never a\n   220\t  wait.\n   221\t\n   222\t  Three diagnostics are the library's own, and they also never wait: an\n   223\t  entry whose `:resolve` answers nil or without a `:stream` wakes with\n   224\t  `:status :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved`; an entry whose `:reason` is\n   225\t  neither `:next` nor `:put` wakes with\n   226\t  `:status :dao.stream.waitset/unsupported-reason`; and a poll that\n   227\t  answers something other than an outcome map — or throws, because its\n   228\t  handle fails the protocol it was resolved to — wakes with\n   229\t  `:status :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer`, the entry no longer\n   230\t  being one that can be trusted to poll. In every case no `:advance` is\n   231\t  called, the store is unchanged, the original entry is returned intact,\n   232\t  and the entry leaves `:waiting`.\n   233\t\n   234\t  Resolvers must not throw. A resolver throw voids the sweep: `check`\n   235\t  does not return, and a caller that re-threads its previous waitset\n   236\t  into a later call re-polls every entry — re-appending writers that\n   237\t  already appended. The engine's sweep behaves identically, and this is\n   238\t  stated as the contract on resolvers rather than caught: host\n   239\t  composition failing is a host defect the library cannot name, and\n   240\t  wrapping it would hide it inside a diagnostic.\"\n   241\t  [waitset resolver store]\n   242\t  (let [{resolve-fn :resolve, advance-fn :advance} resolver]\n   243\t    (loop [remaining (:waiting waitset)\n   244\t           store store\n   245\t           waiting []\n   246\t           woken []]\n   247\t      (if (seq remaining)\n   248\t        (let [entry (first remaining)\n   249\t              reason (:reason entry)]\n   250\t          (if (contains? wait-reasons reason)\n   251\t            (let [resolved (resolve-fn store entry)]\n   252\t              (if (and (map? resolved) (:stream resolved))\n   253\t                (if-some [{:keys [wake advance-cursor]}\n   254\t                          (case reason\n   255\t                            :next (poll-next entry resolved)\n   256\t                            :put (poll-put entry resolved))]\n   257\t                  (recur (rest remaining)\n   258\t                         (cond-> store\n   259\t                           advance-cursor (advance-fn entry advance-cursor))\n   260\t                         waiting\n   261\t                         (conj woken wake))\n   262\t                  (recur (rest remaining)\n   263\t                         store\n   264\t                         (conj waiting entry)\n   265\t                         woken))\n   266\t                (recur (rest remaining)\n   267\t                       store\n   268\t                       waiting\n   269\t                       (conj woken (diagnostic-result\n   270\t                                     entry\n   271\t                                     :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved)))))\n   272\t            (recur (rest remaining)\n   273\t                   store\n   274\t                   waiting\n   275\t                   (conj woken (diagnostic-result\n   276\t                                 entry\n   277\t                                 :dao.stream.waitset/unsupported-reason)))))\n   278\t        {:waitset {:waiting waiting}\n   279\t         :woken woken\n   280\t         :store store}))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '235,300p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '260,290p;405,433p;1290,1365p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '235,300p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '260,290p;405,433p;1290,1365p'\"","aggregated_output":"   235\t(defn qualified-keyword?*\n   236\t  \"True if x is a qualified keyword. Portable across CLJ, CLJS, CLJD.\"\n   237\t  [x]\n   238\t  (boolean (and (keyword? x) (namespace x))))\n   239\t\n   240\t\n   241\t(defn outcome-map?\n   242\t  \"True if x is an open outcome map with a qualified keyword under :dao.stream/outcome.\"\n   243\t  [x]\n   244\t  (and (map? x)\n   245\t       (qualified-keyword?* (:dao.stream/outcome x))))\n   246\t\n   247\t\n   248\t(defn validate-outcome\n   249\t  \"Validates an operation outcome against contract requirements.\n   250\t   Returns nil if valid, or a failure map describing the defect if invalid.\"\n   251\t  [op result]\n   252\t  (if-let [canon-op (canonical-op op)]\n   253\t    (cond\n   254\t      (not (outcome-map? result))\n   255\t      {:valid? false :error :invalid-outcome-map :operation canon-op :result result}\n   256\t\n   257\t      :else\n   258\t      (let [outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   259\t            allowed (get operation-outcomes canon-op)]\n   260\t        (cond\n   261\t          (not (contains? allowed outcome))\n   262\t          {:valid? false\n   263\t           :error :unauthorized-outcome\n   264\t           :operation canon-op\n   265\t           :outcome outcome\n   266\t           :allowed allowed}\n   267\t\n   268\t          :else\n   269\t          (let [req-keys (get outcome-required-keys [canon-op outcome] #{})\n   270\t                missing (into #{} (remove #(contains? result %) req-keys))]\n   271\t            (if (seq missing)\n   272\t              {:valid? false\n   273\t               :error :missing-required-keys\n   274\t               :operation canon-op\n   275\t               :outcome outcome\n   276\t               :missing missing}\n   277\t              nil)))))\n   278\t    {:valid? false :error :unknown-operation :operation op}))\n   279\t\n   280\t\n   281\t(defn valid-outcome?\n   282\t  \"True if result is a valid outcome map for op according to the contract.\"\n   283\t  [op result]\n   284\t  (nil? (validate-outcome op result)))\n   285\t\n   286\t\n   287\t(defn valid-envelope?\n   288\t  \"Generic envelope validation: envelope must be a map with a qualified keyword\n   289\t   under :dao.stream/type. Everything else is transport-owned.\"\n   290\t  [envelope]\n   291\t  (and (map? envelope)\n   292\t       (qualified-keyword?* (:dao.stream/type envelope))))\n   293\t\n   294\t\n   295\t(defn valid-creation-spec?\n   296\t  \"True if spec is a generically valid creation specification envelope:\n   297\t   must be a map with a qualified keyword under :dao.stream/type.\"\n   298\t  [spec]\n   299\t  (valid-envelope? spec))\n   300\t\n   260\t  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)]\n   261\t    (when (nil? secret)\n   262\t      (fail (str \"This task has no capability secret, so it cannot issue\"\n   263\t                 \" a resource reference\")\n   264\t            {:reason :no-capability-secret, :kind kind, :id id}))\n   265\t    {:type kind, :id id, :seal (seal-of secret kind id)}))\n   266\t\n   267\t\n   268\t(defn authentic-ref?\n   269\t  \"True when `ref` is a `kind` reference this task issued: its seal\n   270\t   verifies under the task's secret, and it names a live resource of that\n   271\t   kind.\"\n   272\t  [state kind ref]\n   273\t  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)\n   274\t        id (when (map? ref) (:id ref))\n   275\t        resource (get (:resources state) id)]\n   276\t    (boolean (and (some? secret)\n   277\t                  (= kind (:type ref))\n   278\t                  (some? resource)\n   279\t                  (= (:seal ref) (seal-of secret kind id))\n   280\t                  (= (= :cursor-ref kind) (cursor-cell? resource))))))\n   281\t\n   282\t\n   283\t(defn check-ref!\n   284\t  \"The resource id a program-supplied `kind` reference names, once its\n   285\t   seal verifies (r10). Anything else fails closed with\n   286\t   `:forged-resource-reference`, naming the effect and the id it named.\"\n   287\t  [state effect-kind kind ref]\n   288\t  (if (authentic-ref? state kind ref)\n   289\t    (:id ref)\n   290\t    (fail \"Forged resource reference\"\n   405\t\n   406\t(defn handle-put\n   407\t  \"Handle :stream/put. Total over the six append outcomes, once the\n   408\t   reference verifies.\n   409\t   Returns {:value v :state s} on success, {:park true :stream-id id :state s}\n   410\t   on `full`. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are errors\n   411\t   that name their outcome, as v1's throw on a closed stream did.\n   412\t   `refused`, and any outcome outside this contract version, is not a\n   413\t   throw: the effect's value is the shaped refusal\n   414\t   {:status :refused, :outcome o, :stream-id id} -- data the program\n   415\t   reads, the way `poll-link-response` answers a refused link.\"\n   416\t  [state effect]\n   417\t  (let [stream-id (check-ref! state :stream/put :stream-ref (:stream effect))\n   418\t        val (:val effect)\n   419\t        handle (get (:resources state) stream-id)\n   420\t        result (stream/append! handle val)\n   421\t        o (outcome result)]\n   422\t    (case o\n   423\t      :dao.stream/ok {:value val, :state state}\n   424\t      :dao.stream/full {:park true, :stream-id stream-id, :state state}\n   425\t      (:dao.stream/closed\n   426\t        :dao.stream/invalid-value\n   427\t        :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   428\t      (fail \"Stream append failed\" {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id})\n   429\t      {:state state,\n   430\t       :value {:status :refused, :outcome o, :stream-id stream-id}})))\n   431\t\n   432\t\n   433\t(defn handle-cursor\n  1290\t                                  :link-id link-id})))\n  1291\t      state)))\n  1292\t\n  1293\t\n  1294\t(defn abandon-installs\n  1295\t  \"A composition gives up on every install in flight (the abandoned case\n  1296\t   of section 7.2, step 7, reaching section 7.3's `refused`): each child\n  1297\t   is dropped -- nothing from its store is published -- and every\n  1298\t   `:install` waiter is restored with the reason as the require's error,\n  1299\t   so the install entry is removed and a later require may try again. A\n  1300\t   state with no installs is unchanged. The dropped children's own link\n  1301\t   ids are never minted again by a state that carries `:origins` forward\n  1302\t   past them.\"\n  1303\t  [state reason]\n  1304\t  (reduce (fn [state module-name]\n  1305\t            (refuse-install state module-name\n  1306\t                            {:status :lost, :reason reason}))\n  1307\t          state\n  1308\t          (keys (:installs state))))\n  1309\t\n  1310\t\n  1311\t(defn check-wait-set\n  1312\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n  1313\t\n  1314\t   The sweep is `dao.stream.waitset/check` — this engine was that library's\n  1315\t   seed and is now its first consumer. Every entry is resolved to a live\n  1316\t   handle and an opaque cursor out of the store by `waitset-resolver` and\n  1317\t   polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution happens per\n  1318\t   poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n  1319\t   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n  1320\t\n  1321\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n  1322\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n  1323\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values —\n  1324\t   the value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next —\n  1325\t   instead of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll\n  1326\t   cursor. An entry that stays waiting is retained in its stored,\n  1327\t   resource-id form.\n  1328\t\n  1329\t   Each woken result's `:status` is stamped onto its ready entry, where\n  1330\t   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n  1331\t   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n  1332\t   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n  1333\t   there too, before any continuation is restored.\n  1334\t\n  1335\t   The link wait entries `require` adds (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.2) are\n  1336\t   polled first, by the engine rather than the stream sweep: a\n  1337\t   `:link-request` retries its envelope, a `:link-response` advances its\n  1338\t   own kept cursor to the response carrying its id, and an `:install`\n  1339\t   waits on its install child. The install children are then stepped in\n  1340\t   this same round (section 7.3), so a child that halts restores its\n  1341\t   waiters here; nothing about a child runs inside a restore.\"\n  1342\t  [state]\n  1343\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n  1344\t    (if (and (empty? wait-set) (empty? (:installs state)))\n  1345\t      state\n  1346\t      (let [state (poll-links state\n  1347\t                              (filterv link-entry? wait-set)\n  1348\t                              (remove link-entry? wait-set))\n  1349\t            wait-set (:wait-set state)\n  1350\t            streams (filterv (complement link-entry?) wait-set)\n  1351\t            {:keys [woken store], :as result}\n  1352\t            (waitset/check {:waiting streams}\n  1353\t                           waitset-resolver\n  1354\t                           (:resources state))\n  1355\t            v (assoc state\n  1356\t                     :resources store\n  1357\t                     :wait-set (into (:waiting (:waitset result))\n  1358\t                                     (filter link-entry?)\n  1359\t                                     wait-set))]\n  1360\t        (update v\n  1361\t                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n  1362\t                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n  1363\t\n  1364\t\n  1365\t(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
